<!-- ── Debrief (shown after completion) ── -->
<div class="lab-debrief" id="lab-debrief">
  <div class="lab-debrief-label">Debrief — JWT Algorithm Confusion</div>

  <h3>What happened</h3>
  <p>DevPortal's JWT verification code calls <code>jwt.verify()</code> without specifying which algorithms are acceptable. This allows the attacker to set the <code>alg</code> header to <code>"none"</code>, which tells the library to skip signature verification entirely. The attacker can then modify the payload (changing their role to admin) and the server accepts the forged token as valid.</p>

  <h3>The vulnerable code</h3>
  <pre>const decoded = jwt.verify(token, publicKey);
// No algorithms option — trusts whatever alg the token header specifies
// alg:"none" = no signature required</pre>

  <h3>The fix</h3>
  <p>Always specify the expected algorithm when verifying JWTs. Pass an <code>algorithms</code> allowlist to the verification function. Never allow <code>"none"</code> in production. Additionally, validate the <code>iss</code> (issuer), <code>aud</code> (audience), and <code>exp</code> (expiration) claims.</p>
  <pre>// Enforce algorithm — only accept RS256
const decoded = jwt.verify(token, publicKey, {
  algorithms: ['RS256'],
  issuer: 'devportal.io',
  audience: 'api.devportal.io',
});

// Reject: alg:"none", alg:"HS256" (symmetric with public key)
// Also: rotate signing keys, use short expiration times</pre>

  <h3>Real-world impact</h3>
  <p>JWT algorithm confusion allows an attacker to forge tokens for any user or role. With an admin token, they can access any account, modify data, create backdoor accounts, or exfiltrate the entire database. This attack requires no knowledge of the server's private key.</p>
  <p><strong>Auth0 jsonwebtoken (CVE-2015-9235):</strong> The popular Node.js JWT library accepted tokens with <code>alg: "none"</code> by default, allowing attackers to forge valid tokens without any cryptographic key. The vulnerability affected thousands of applications before the library was patched to reject <code>"none"</code> unless explicitly opted in.</p>

  <h3>How to find this in the wild</h3>
  <p>Intercept a valid JWT (from login or API calls), decode the header, and change <code>alg</code> to <code>"none"</code>. Remove the signature (everything after the second dot) and replay the request. If the server accepts it, the vulnerability is confirmed. Also test <code>alg: "HS256"</code> with the public key as the HMAC secret — another common JWT confusion attack.</p>

